<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="26b652b8">&lt;p&gt;
    A Benefits Dependency Map typically illustrates the many-to-many relationships between enablers, required changes,
    benefits and objectives. The enablers are related to the identified benefits via the either business or enabling
    changes. Business changes are the new ways of working that are required so that the desired benefits are realized.
    Enabling changes are changes that are fundamentals for achieving the business changes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Key components of Benefits Dependency Map generally include the following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.bus.common.base/workproducts/./resources/BDM Table_Figure 1.jpg&quot; width=&quot;422&quot; height=&quot;352&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="59457d30"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="ad2be313">&lt;p&gt;
    The creation of the network requires knowledge to be shared among business managers, key stakeholders and SMRs. It is
    important that they all understand what the benefits are and how realizing each of the benefits depends on specific
    changes that need to be made.  It is an iterative process to develop such networks, since as required changes are
    identified, a network of interrelating changes and benefits will evolve, and the feasibility of achieving some of the
    benefits will be questioned.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    From the network it should be explicitly understood how each benefit relates to one or more of the investment
    objectives, who owns it and will drive through the realization and whether, and perhaps how, it can be measured.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The following list shows some sample financial and non-financial benefits that can be considered while
    building out the Benefits Dependency Map:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Cost saving
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Cost avoidance
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Increased revenue
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Productivity
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Effectiveness
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Quality
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Innovation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Regulatory
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Client satisfaction
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Employee satisfaction
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="7953abd8"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="e1879711">Benefits Dependency Maps can be a powerful way to engage with stakeholders to identify benefits and dis-benefits arising
from the introduction of a change. They can be used to identify key dependencies and serve as a method for prioritizing the
most significant benefits that should be pursued and measured. Understanding how benefits relate to investment objectives,
who owns what will be the starting points for building and refining the business case.</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="b826e2ca"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="d27582c7"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="3e4b65bd"></attribute>
<attribute name="representationOptions" nodeId="4c3491c9">&lt;p&gt;
    The Benefits Dependency Map shows the investment objectives, their resulting benefits and how they&apos;re linked in a
    structured way to the business, organizational and IS/IT changes required realizing those benefits.  Changes
    and benefits are shown as boxes on the diagram with enablers and objectives as circles with lines denoting the
    relationships.  The shapes are normally aligned in columns based on what they represent. 
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
     &lt;img alt=&quot;Benefit Dependency Map&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.bus.common.base/workproducts/resources/benefit_depend_map.gif&quot; width=&quot;612&quot; height=&quot;546&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="representation" nodeId="c48bcf1a"></attribute>
<attribute name="notation" nodeId="ca3c64c"></attribute>
</Element>
